23 million pounds to end hiv in this  country by 2030 is that the right  approach do you think  well i think that we can do that  if you look at places of high prevalence  at like brighton we had 20  or so  hiv diagnosis this year but only four of  them were newly infected the rest were  people who have had it for over a year  or longer and just weren't getting  tested so this real push to make sure  that we test and in brighton and  manchester and a few other areas we're  going to do opt-out testing now so when  you get your bloods done we'll  automatically screen it for hiv we can  really start to push to get um  no more infections in this country and  start to end the disease but we only do  that by also starting to end stigma and  as your report said the the fastest  growing group of people for diagnostic  infections are actually women at the  moment so we need to make sure that  we're reaching out to all parts and all  sections of our society because we  really can do this it is in our grasp  it's taken 40 years but isn't it amazing  that human endeavor has meant that  within 40 years we might be able to  within 50 years we might be able to  actually eradicate this disease in  britain first of all and then around the  world and for those people in this  country who are identified as hiv  positive  am i right in saying now medical  treatment is such that you know you can  live with it and live a normal life it's  not  if it's identified it's not the deadly  disease that we will remember from the  80s and 90s  exactly i mean i'm i've been hiv  positive now for 10 years i'm on  treatment i take one pill a day just  before i go to bed and when they test my  blood it's undetectable it means the  test can't even detect hiv there if i  stop taking the pill it might come back  but actually that undetectable means i  can't pass it on which is vitally  important for if we're going to stop  this disease but it also means apart  from um you know kind of my usual  activities uh you know kind of that we  all do that might limit our lives you  know kind of over drinking and smoking  or whatever that people do i will live  as long as anyone else and what about  the rest of the world away from the rich  world i mean are  is that sort of treatment that sort of  maintenance treatment available  uh in the places where hiv positivity is  high you know for example in in africa  well unfortunately in africa hiv and  aids is still the biggest killer for  adolescent girls so you know there's a  stark difference of course and we need  to roll these medications out i mean  i've been on trips where um they will  have part of the medication but not the  whole amount of medication  but in places like africa it's not just  about dealing with hiv it's dealing with  health in a holistic way because if you  haven't got that the centers health  centers across the country you haven't  got places where people can get tested  where people can pick up their medicine  so it's not just about attacking hiv  alone it's about tackling health  issues in those countries and then it  has advantages for tb malaria hiv all  those diseases can be started to be  tackled but  this plan is primarily about in the uk  how we can try and eradicate it in less  than 10 years how we can make sure we  stack tackle the stigma internal stigma  of people that might have it that means  that they then don't get treated or  tested but also the societal stigma in  terms of making sure that we they know  and everyone knows that if you are on  treatment you're undetectable if you're  undetectable you can't pass it on and  you live that life and that will make  the real difference and if we can show  that we can do it here in the richer  countries then the focus will need to  pass on to us helping the poorer  countries do that as well now it is  world aids day that's your ribbon you're  wearing in your lapel  and in advance of that uh joe biden has  made a significant statement uh saying  t